{info:title=Azure Dynamic Key Rotation with HashiCorp Vault}
This guide covers implementing dynamic Azure credential generation using HashiCorp Vault's Azure secrets engine to eliminate static service principal secrets and enhance security through short-lived credentials.
{info}

{panel:title=Page Information|borderStyle=dashed|borderColor=#ccc|titleBGColor=#d9ead3|bgColor=#f9f9f9}
*Target Audience:* Cloud Engineers, Azure Administrators, DevOps Engineers
*Estimated Reading Time:* 12-15 minutes
*Prerequisites:* Azure subscription with admin access, HashiCorp Vault cluster
*Related Pages:* [Keystore Management Guide|03-keystore-management-guide], [Database Dynamic Key Rotation|05-database-dynamic-key-rotation]
{panel}

h1. Table of Contents

{toc:printable=true|style=square|maxLevel=3|indent=10px|minLevel=1|exclude=Table of Contents}

h1. Overview

Azure dynamic key rotation with HashiCorp Vault generates short-lived Azure credentials on-demand, eliminating static service principal secrets and reducing security risks through automated credential lifecycle management.

h2. Key Benefits

{panel:title=Dynamic Credential Benefits|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#0078d4|titleBGColor=#106ebe|titleColor=#ffffff}
• *Enhanced Security:* Short-lived credentials (minutes to hours)
• *Zero Static Storage:* No credentials stored in applications  
• *Automatic Rotation:* Credentials expire and rotate automatically
• *Complete Audit Trail:* Full visibility into credential usage
• *Principle of Least Privilege:* Granular Azure role assignments
• *Compliance Ready:* Automated governance and access controls
{panel}

h1. Prerequisites

h2. Azure Requirements

{warning:title=Required Azure Permissions}
Service principal for Vault requires these specific permissions:
{warning}

{code:language=json|title=Required Azure Permissions|borderStyle=solid}
{
  "permissions": [
    "Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/*",
    "Microsoft.Authorization/roleDefinitions/read", 
    "Microsoft.Resources/subscriptions/resourceGroups/read"
  ]
}
{code}

{tip:title=Azure Setup Checklist}
✓ Azure subscription with admin access
✓ Service principal created for Vault integration
✓ Required permissions assigned to service principal
✓ Resource groups identified for role scope assignments
✓ Network connectivity from Vault to Azure APIs verified
{tip}

h2. Vault Requirements

{panel:title=Vault Prerequisites|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#27ae60|titleBGColor=#2ecc71|titleColor=#ffffff}
• Vault cluster with administrative access
• Network connectivity to Azure Management APIs
• Appropriate Vault policies for Azure secrets engine
• Monitoring and audit logging configured
{panel}

h1. Azure Secrets Engine Setup

h2. 1. Enable and Configure Azure Engine

{code:language=bash|title=Azure Secrets Engine Configuration|borderStyle=solid}
# Enable Azure secrets engine
vault secrets enable azure

# Configure Azure connection with service principal
vault write azure/config \
    subscription_id="12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012" \
    tenant_id="87654321-4321-4321-4321-210987654321" \
    client_id="vault-service-principal-id" \
    client_secret="vault-sp-secret" \
    environment="AzurePublicCloud"

# Verify configuration (client_secret will be redacted)
vault read azure/config
{code}

{note:title=Environment Options}
Supported Azure environments:
• {{AzurePublicCloud}} (default)
• {{AzureUSGovernmentCloud}}
• {{AzureChinaCloud}}
• {{AzureGermanCloud}}
{note}

h2. 2. Create Azure Role Definitions

{expand:title=Read-Only Monitoring Role}
{code:language=bash|title=Read-Only Role Configuration|borderStyle=solid}
vault write azure/roles/readonly \
    azure_roles='[
        {
            "role_name": "Reader",
            "scope": "/subscriptions/12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012/resourceGroups/monitoring-rg"
        }
    ]' \
    ttl="1h" \
    max_ttl="24h"
{code}
{expand}

{expand:title=Storage Administrator Role}
{code:language=bash|title=Storage Admin Role Configuration|borderStyle=solid}
vault write azure/roles/storage-admin \
    azure_roles='[
        {
            "role_name": "Storage Account Contributor",
            "scope": "/subscriptions/12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012/resourceGroups/storage-rg"
        },
        {
            "role_name": "Storage Blob Data Contributor", 
            "scope": "/subscriptions/12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012/resourceGroups/storage-rg"
        }
    ]' \
    ttl="30m" \
    max_ttl="2h"
{code}
{expand}

{expand:title=Application Deployment Role}
{code:language=bash|title=App Deployer Role Configuration|borderStyle=solid}
vault write azure/roles/app-deployer \
    azure_roles='[
        {
            "role_name": "Contributor",
            "scope": "/subscriptions/12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012/resourceGroups/app-rg"
        }
    ]' \
    ttl="4h" \
    max_ttl="12h"
{code}
{expand}

h2. 3. Role Configuration Best Practices

{panel:title=Role Design Guidelines|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#e67e22|titleBGColor=#f39c12|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Scope Granularity:* Use resource group or resource-level scopes instead of subscription-wide
*TTL Strategy:* 
• Development: 1-4 hours
• CI/CD: 15-30 minutes  
• Production: 30 minutes - 2 hours
• Monitoring: 1-24 hours

*Role Separation:* Create specific roles for different use cases rather than broad permissions
{panel}

h1. Dynamic Credential Management

h2. 1. Generate Azure Credentials

{code:language=bash|title=Credential Generation Examples|borderStyle=solid}
# Generate credentials for specific role
vault read azure/creds/readonly

# Example output:
# Key                Value
# ---                -----
# lease_id           azure/creds/readonly/abc123def456
# lease_duration     3600
# lease_renewable    true
# client_id          a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-1234-567890abcdef
# client_secret      generated-client-secret-value

# Store credentials in variables for automation
AZURE_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json azure/creds/readonly)
CLIENT_ID=$(echo $AZURE_CREDS | jq -r '.data.client_id')
CLIENT_SECRET=$(echo $AZURE_CREDS | jq -r '.data.client_secret')
LEASE_ID=$(echo $AZURE_CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')

echo "Generated Azure credentials with lease: $LEASE_ID"
{code}

h2. 2. Lease Management Operations

{code:language=bash|title=Lease Management Commands|borderStyle=solid}
# Renew lease with default increment
vault lease renew $LEASE_ID

# Renew lease with specific duration (1 hour)
vault lease renew -increment=3600 $LEASE_ID

# Revoke credentials before expiration
vault lease revoke $LEASE_ID

# Revoke all leases for a specific role
vault lease revoke -prefix azure/creds/readonly

# List active leases for monitoring
vault list sys/leases/lookup/azure/creds/readonly

# Get lease information
vault lease lookup $LEASE_ID
{code}

{warning:title=Lease Management Best Practices}
• Always implement proper lease renewal logic in applications
• Monitor lease expiration times and renew proactively
• Implement graceful fallback when credential generation fails
• Revoke credentials when no longer needed to minimize exposure
{warning}

h1. Application Integration

h2. 1. Python Integration Example

{expand:title=Complete Python Implementation}
{code:language=python|title=Azure Vault Client Python Implementation|borderStyle=solid}
import hvac
import json
import time
from azure.identity import ClientSecretCredential
from azure.mgmt.resource import ResourceManagementClient
from azure.mgmt.storage import StorageManagementClient

class AzureVaultClient:
    def __init__(self, vault_url, vault_token, subscription_id, tenant_id):
        """Initialize Azure Vault client with connection details"""
        self.vault_client = hvac.Client(url=vault_url, token=vault_token)
        self.subscription_id = subscription_id
        self.tenant_id = tenant_id
        self.current_credentials = {}
        
    def get_azure_credentials(self, role_name, force_refresh=False):
        """Get fresh Azure credentials from Vault with caching"""
        # Check if we have cached credentials that are still valid
        if not force_refresh and role_name in self.current_credentials:
            lease_info = self.vault_client.sys.lookup_lease(
                self.current_credentials[role_name]['lease_id']
            )
            if lease_info['data']['ttl'] > 300:  # 5 minutes buffer
                return self.current_credentials[role_name]
        
        # Get fresh credentials from Vault
        response = self.vault_client.read(f'azure/creds/{role_name}')
        if not response:
            raise Exception(f"Failed to get credentials for role: {role_name}")
            
        credentials = {
            'client_id': response['data']['client_id'],
            'client_secret': response['data']['client_secret'],
            'lease_id': response['lease_id'],
            'lease_duration': response['lease_duration']
        }
        
        # Cache credentials
        self.current_credentials[role_name] = credentials
        return credentials
    
    def get_azure_credential(self, role_name):
        """Get Azure ClientSecretCredential object"""
        creds = self.get_azure_credentials(role_name)
        return ClientSecretCredential(
            tenant_id=self.tenant_id,
            client_id=creds['client_id'],
            client_secret=creds['client_secret']
        )
    
    def get_resource_client(self, role_name):
        """Get authenticated Azure Resource Management client"""
        credential = self.get_azure_credential(role_name)
        return ResourceManagementClient(credential, self.subscription_id)
    
    def get_storage_client(self, role_name):
        """Get authenticated Azure Storage Management client"""
        credential = self.get_azure_credential(role_name)
        return StorageManagementClient(credential, self.subscription_id)
    
    def revoke_credentials(self, role_name):
        """Revoke cached credentials"""
        if role_name in self.current_credentials:
            lease_id = self.current_credentials[role_name]['lease_id']
            self.vault_client.sys.revoke_lease(lease_id)
            del self.current_credentials[role_name]

# Usage example
def main():
    vault_client = AzureVaultClient(
        vault_url="https://vault.example.com:8200",
        vault_token="hvs.CAESIJ...",
        subscription_id="12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012",
        tenant_id="87654321-4321-4321-4321-210987654321"
    )
    
    try:
        # Get authenticated Azure client
        azure_client = vault_client.get_resource_client('readonly')
        
        # List resource groups
        print("Resource Groups:")
        for rg in azure_client.resource_groups.list():
            print(f"  - {rg.name} ({rg.location})")
            
        # Get storage client for storage operations
        storage_client = vault_client.get_storage_client('storage-admin')
        
        # List storage accounts
        print("\nStorage Accounts:")
        for account in storage_client.storage_accounts.list():
            print(f"  - {account.name}")
            
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"Error: {e}")
    finally:
        # Clean up credentials
        vault_client.revoke_credentials('readonly')
        vault_client.revoke_credentials('storage-admin')

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
{code}
{expand}

h2. 2. Shell Script Integration

{code:language=bash|title=Bash Integration Script|borderStyle=solid}
#!/bin/bash
# azure-deploy.sh - Azure deployment with Vault dynamic credentials

set -euo pipefail

# Configuration
VAULT_ADDR="${VAULT_ADDR:-https://vault.example.com:8200}"
AZURE_ROLE="${AZURE_ROLE:-app-deployer}"
SUBSCRIPTION_ID="12345678-1234-1234-1234-123456789012"
TENANT_ID="87654321-4321-4321-4321-210987654321"

# Function to get Azure credentials from Vault
get_azure_credentials() {
    local role_name="$1"
    local creds_json
    
    echo "Getting Azure credentials for role: $role_name" >&2
    creds_json=$(vault read -format=json "azure/creds/$role_name")
    
    if [[ -z "$creds_json" ]]; then
        echo "Failed to get credentials from Vault" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    
    echo "$creds_json"
}

# Function to authenticate with Azure CLI
azure_login() {
    local creds_json="$1"
    local client_id client_secret lease_id
    
    client_id=$(echo "$creds_json" | jq -r '.data.client_id')
    client_secret=$(echo "$creds_json" | jq -r '.data.client_secret')
    lease_id=$(echo "$creds_json" | jq -r '.lease_id')
    
    echo "Authenticating with Azure CLI..." >&2
    az login --service-principal \
        --username "$client_id" \
        --password "$client_secret" \
        --tenant "$TENANT_ID" \
        --output none
    
    az account set --subscription "$SUBSCRIPTION_ID"
    
    # Return lease ID for cleanup
    echo "$lease_id"
}

# Function to cleanup credentials
cleanup_credentials() {
    local lease_id="$1"
    
    if [[ -n "$lease_id" ]]; then
        echo "Revoking Vault lease: $lease_id" >&2
        vault lease revoke "$lease_id" || true
    fi
    
    echo "Logging out of Azure CLI..." >&2
    az logout || true
}

# Main deployment function
deploy_application() {
    local resource_group="$1"
    local template_file="$2"
    local parameters_file="$3"
    
    echo "Deploying application to resource group: $resource_group"
    
    az deployment group create \
        --resource-group "$resource_group" \
        --template-file "$template_file" \
        --parameters "@$parameters_file" \
        --output table
}

# Main script execution
main() {
    local creds_json lease_id
    
    # Get credentials from Vault
    creds_json=$(get_azure_credentials "$AZURE_ROLE")
    
    # Authenticate with Azure
    lease_id=$(azure_login "$creds_json")
    
    # Set up cleanup trap
    trap "cleanup_credentials '$lease_id'" EXIT
    
    # Perform deployment operations
    deploy_application "my-app-rg" "azuredeploy.json" "parameters.json"
    
    echo "Deployment completed successfully!"
}

# Execute main function
main "$@"
{code}

h2. 3. Terraform Integration

{expand:title=Terraform Provider Configuration}
{code:language=hcl|title=Terraform Azure Provider with Vault|borderStyle=solid}
# Configure Vault provider
terraform {
  required_providers {
    vault = {
      source  = "hashicorp/vault"
      version = "~> 3.0"
    }
    azurerm = {
      source  = "hashicorp/azurerm"
      version = "~> 3.0"
    }
  }
}

# Configure Vault provider
provider "vault" {
  address = var.vault_address
  token   = var.vault_token
}

# Get Azure credentials from Vault
data "vault_generic_secret" "azure_creds" {
  path = "azure/creds/${var.azure_role}"
}

# Configure Azure provider with dynamic credentials
provider "azurerm" {
  features {}
  
  subscription_id = var.subscription_id
  tenant_id       = var.tenant_id
  client_id       = data.vault_generic_secret.azure_creds.data["client_id"]
  client_secret   = data.vault_generic_secret.azure_creds.data["client_secret"]
}

# Example resource using dynamic credentials
resource "azurerm_resource_group" "example" {
  name     = "terraform-example-rg"
  location = "East US"
  
  tags = {
    environment = "development"
    managed_by  = "terraform"
    vault_lease = data.vault_generic_secret.azure_creds.lease_id
  }
}

# Output lease information for cleanup
output "vault_lease_id" {
  value     = data.vault_generic_secret.azure_creds.lease_id
  sensitive = true
}
{code}
{expand}

h1. Monitoring and Best Practices

h2. 1. Monitoring Azure Dynamic Credentials

{panel:title=Key Monitoring Metrics|borderStyle=solid|borderColor=#e74c3c|titleBGColor=#c0392b|titleColor=#ffffff}
*Credential Usage:*
• Number of credentials generated per role
• Credential generation failure rates
• Average credential lifetime and usage patterns

*Lease Management:*
• Lease renewal success/failure rates
• Expired vs. revoked credential ratios
• Time between generation and first use

*Security Events:*
• Failed authentication attempts with dynamic credentials
• Unusual access patterns or times
• Credentials used from unexpected locations
{panel}

h2. 2. Performance Optimization

{tip:title=Performance Best Practices}
*Credential Caching:* Implement client-side caching with proper TTL handling
*Parallel Operations:* Use connection pooling for multiple Azure API calls
*Renewal Strategy:* Renew credentials before 80% of TTL expires
*Error Handling:* Implement exponential backoff for credential generation failures
{tip}

h2. 3. Security Best Practices

{warning:title=Security Considerations}
*Principle of Least Privilege:* Grant minimal required Azure permissions
*Network Security:* Restrict Vault and Azure API access to authorized networks
*Audit Logging:* Enable comprehensive logging for credential lifecycle events
*Regular Review:* Periodically review role definitions and access patterns
{warning}

h2. 4. Troubleshooting Common Issues

{expand:title=Credential Generation Failures}
*Symptoms:* {{vault read azure/creds/role-name}} returns errors

*Common Causes:*
• Service principal permissions insufficient
• Azure subscription or tenant ID incorrect
• Network connectivity issues to Azure APIs
• Vault Azure secrets engine misconfiguration

*Solutions:*
1. Verify service principal permissions in Azure portal
2. Test Azure API connectivity from Vault server
3. Check Vault logs for detailed error messages
4. Validate Azure secrets engine configuration
{expand}

{expand:title=Authentication Failures}
*Symptoms:* Azure CLI or SDK authentication fails with generated credentials

*Common Causes:*
• Credentials expired before use
• Role assignments not yet propagated in Azure
• Incorrect tenant or subscription ID
• Network restrictions blocking Azure API access

*Solutions:*
1. Check credential TTL and generation time
2. Wait for Azure role propagation (up to 5 minutes)
3. Verify tenant and subscription configuration
4. Test network connectivity to Azure endpoints
{expand}

{expand:title=Lease Management Issues}
*Symptoms:* Lease renewal failures or unexpected revocations

*Common Causes:*
• Network connectivity issues during renewal
• Vault token expired or insufficient permissions
• Lease ID incorrect or already revoked

*Solutions:*
1. Implement retry logic for lease operations
2. Monitor Vault token TTL and renewal
3. Validate lease ID format and existence
4. Check Vault audit logs for lease events
{expand}

h1. Integration Examples

h2. CI/CD Pipeline Integration

{code:language=yaml|title=Azure DevOps Pipeline Example|borderStyle=solid}
# azure-pipelines.yml
trigger:
- main

pool:
  vmImage: 'ubuntu-latest'

variables:
  VAULT_ADDR: 'https://vault.example.com:8200'
  AZURE_ROLE: 'app-deployer'

steps:
- task: AzureCLI@2
  displayName: 'Get Vault Credentials and Deploy'
  inputs:
    azureSubscription: 'vault-dynamic-connection'
    scriptType: 'bash'
    scriptLocation: 'inlineScript'
    inlineScript: |
      # Authenticate with Vault using CI/CD token
      export VAULT_TOKEN="$(VAULT_CI_TOKEN)"
      
      # Get dynamic Azure credentials
      CREDS=$(vault read -format=json azure/creds/$AZURE_ROLE)
      CLIENT_ID=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.data.client_id')
      CLIENT_SECRET=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.data.client_secret')
      LEASE_ID=$(echo $CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')
      
      # Authenticate with Azure
      az login --service-principal \
        --username $CLIENT_ID \
        --password $CLIENT_SECRET \
        --tenant $(AZURE_TENANT_ID)
      
      # Deploy resources
      az deployment group create \
        --resource-group myapp-rg \
        --template-file infrastructure/main.bicep \
        --parameters @infrastructure/parameters.json
      
      # Cleanup
      vault lease revoke $LEASE_ID
{code}

